      "architectural_decisions": "System Design Overview: Key Architectural Decisions\n\nKey decisions driving the design of this R&D software project include:\n\nMulti-Channel Acquisition and Timestamping: The system will acquire telemetry data from at least three sensor channels simultaneously, ensuring comprehensive data capture.  Each sample will be timestamped with high accuracy (within +/- 1 ms) to enable precise time-based analysis.\nReal-Time Data Delivery: A dedicated data buffering module ensures reliable data delivery even under high demand conditions. This buffer capacity guarantees the system can maintain real-time data flow, enabling immediate processing and analysis.\nREST Interface for Ground Segment Access: The system will expose acquired samples through a REST interface to the ground segment, facilitating seamless data transfer and analysis by ground segment operators.\nEngineering Log for Operational Analysis: A detailed engineering log with a minimum depth of six months captures mission operation data, serving as a historical record for troubleshooting and operational analysis. This ensures comprehensive historical context for future operations.\n\nPower Management & Reliability: The system operates within a 28 V DC power envelope not exceeding 45 W, ensuring efficient power consumption and reliable operation.  The system's Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) must be not less than 2000 hours, guaranteeing continuous operation and minimizing downtime.\n\nAccess Control & System Boundaries: Access to acquisition controls is restricted to authenticated operators only, preventing unauthorized manipulation of the system. This ensures data integrity and security within defined boundaries.\n\nThese key architectural decisions ensure a robust and reliable telemetry data acquisition and processing system that meets the project's requirements for real-time analysis, operational history, and secure operation."
    },
